-- title:
--   Theorem 3.5 — stochastic MWU: regret against expected losses and deviations of auxiliary statistics, with probability 1 − δ
-- statement:
--   In stochastic MWU, round $t$ draws a sample $S^t\sim D^t$ and computes losses $\hat\ell^t_i$ and auxiliary statistics $\hat y^t_1,\dots,\hat y^t_k$ from it (the choice of $D^t$, losses and statistics may depend on the history), and updates $W^{t+1}_i=W^t_i\exp(-\eta\hat\ell^t_i)$; $\ell^t_i=E_t[\hat\ell^t_i]$ and $y^t_j=E_t[\hat y^t_j]$ are conditional expectations given the history before round $t$. Suppose all $\hat\ell^t_i$ lie in a common fixed interval of length $B_0>0$ and all $\hat y^t_j$ in one of length $B_1>0$, and let $\delta\in(0,1)$. Then there is a constant $C>0$ such that, with probability at least $1-\delta$, simultaneously
--   $$\frac1T\sum_{t=1}^T\big(\langle\tilde W^t,\hat\ell^t\rangle-\ell^t_i\big)\le C\Big(\frac{\log n}{\eta T}+\eta B_0^2+B_0\sqrt{\frac{\log(n/\delta)}T}\Big)\quad\text{for all }i\in[n],$$
--   $$\frac1T\Big|\sum_{t=1}^T\big(\hat y^t_j-y^t_j\big)\Big|\le C\,B_1\sqrt{\frac{\log(k/\delta)}T}\quad\text{for all }j\in[k].$$
--   The constant $C$ is universal (independent of $n,k,T,\eta,B_0,B_1,\delta$ and the process). This is the engine of the proper algorithm: the losses drive the proxies and the auxiliary statistics are the score estimates.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The process is a probability space with a filtration $(\mathcal F_t)$; round $t$ (0-based) is $\mathcal F_{t+1}$-measurable and conditions on $\mathcal F_t$. The weights are the MWU weights of the sampled losses, computed pathwise.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--
--   - reading: "Consider any δ ∈ (0, 1). Then, there exists a constant C > 0" → ∃ C > 0 before every parameter, δ included (one universal constant); reason: the reading the proof of Theorem 1.1 needs (T = cε⁻²log(n/δ) with one c for all δ) and the stronger one; a C depending on the instance would make the statement nearly empty
--   - reading: rounds with samples S^t ∼ D^t chosen from the history; E_t = expectation given the history before round t → a filtration ℱ; ℓ̂^t_i and ŷ^t_j are ℱ_{t+1}-strongly measurable; ℓ^t_i = P[ℓ̂^t_i | ℱ_t], y^t_j = P[ŷ^t_j | ℱ_t]; ℱ_0 arbitrary; reason: the filtration generated by the history and the samples is one such ℱ; the statement covers every filtration
--   - encoding: common fixed intervals of length B₀ and B₁ → reals c₀, c₁ with ℓ̂^t_i ∈ [c₀, c₀ + B₀] and ŷ^t_j ∈ [c₁, c₁ + B₁] P-almost surely, for every t < T, i and j; reason: the common interval is fixed across rounds and indices; almost-sure bounds retain all bounded random variables, including their null-set modifications
--   - encoding: the inequalities hold with probability 1 − δ → the probability of the set where some inequality fails is at most δ; reason: a lower bound on a possibly non-measurable success set would be read as an outer measure, which is weaker
--   - encoding: rounds t ∈ [T]; items [n], statistics [k] → 0-based rounds t < T; Fin n, Fin k; reason: Lean convention; same instances
-- source:
--   Aamand, Aliakbarpour, Chen, Silwal, Stevens, Zou, A Good Way to Find Good Hypotheses, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 a428fbe0…), p. 16, Theorem 3.5

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_GoodHypotheses_Proper_MWU

open MeasureTheory

namespace GoodHypotheses.Proper

/-- Theorem 3.5 (Stochastic MWU, p. 16), stated as printed, with "there exists a constant
`C > 0`" read as one universal constant. On a probability space with a filtration `ℱ`, round
`t` (`0`-based, `t < T`) has sampled losses `ℓ̂^t_i` and auxiliary statistics `ŷ^t_j` that are
`ℱ_{t+1}`-measurable (functions of the history and the round's sample), almost surely contained in fixed
intervals `[c₀, c₀ + B₀]` and `[c₁, c₁ + B₁]`; the weights are the MWU weights driven by the
sampled losses (pathwise); `ℓ^t_i = E[ℓ̂^t_i | ℱ_t]` and `y^t_j = E[ŷ^t_j | ℱ_t]` condition on the
history before round `t`. Then for every `δ ∈ (0, 1)`, with probability at least `1 − δ`,
simultaneously for all `i ∈ [n]` and `j ∈ [k]`:
`(1/T) ∑_t (⟨W̃^t, ℓ̂^t⟩ − ℓ^t_i) ≤ C (log n/(ηT) + ηB₀² + B₀ √(log(n/δ)/T))` and
`(1/T) |∑_t (ŷ^t_j − y^t_j)| ≤ C B₁ √(log(k/δ)/T)`. -/
theorem theorem_3_5 :
    ∃ C : ℝ, 0 < C ∧
      ∀ (Ω : Type) [m₀ : MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure P]
        (ℱ : Filtration ℕ m₀) (n k T : ℕ) (η B₀ B₁ c₀ c₁ δ : ℝ)
        (ℓhat : ℕ → Fin n → Ω → ℝ) (yhat : ℕ → Fin k → Ω → ℝ),
        1 ≤ n → 1 ≤ k → 1 ≤ T → 0 < η → 0 < B₀ → 0 < B₁ → 0 < δ → δ < 1 →
        (∀ t, t < T → ∀ i, StronglyMeasurable[ℱ (t + 1)] (ℓhat t i)) →
        (∀ t, t < T → ∀ j, StronglyMeasurable[ℱ (t + 1)] (yhat t j)) →
        (∀ t, t < T → ∀ i, ∀ᵐ ω ∂P, c₀ ≤ ℓhat t i ω ∧ ℓhat t i ω ≤ c₀ + B₀) →
        (∀ t, t < T → ∀ j, ∀ᵐ ω ∂P, c₁ ≤ yhat t j ω ∧ yhat t j ω ≤ c₁ + B₁) →
        P {ω | ¬ ((∀ i : Fin n,
              (1 / (T : ℝ)) * ∑ t ∈ Finset.range T,
                  (mixLoss η (fun s i' => ℓhat s i' ω) t - (P[ℓhat t i | ℱ t]) ω)
                ≤ C * (Real.log n / (η * T) + η * B₀ ^ 2
                    + B₀ * Real.sqrt (Real.log (n / δ) / T))) ∧
            (∀ j : Fin k,
              (1 / (T : ℝ)) * |∑ t ∈ Finset.range T, (yhat t j ω - (P[yhat t j | ℱ t]) ω)|
                ≤ C * B₁ * Real.sqrt (Real.log (k / δ) / T)))}
          ≤ ENNReal.ofReal δ := by sorry

end GoodHypotheses.Proper
